          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Wall most commonly symbolizes protection, security, and God-given salvation, while context-specific alternatives include separation, vulnerability, obstacles, judgment, and false security.\",\"book_summary\":\"Wall signifies salvation understood as God's covenantal enclosure and presence; self-built walls of wealth or false peace are counterfeits that fail under judgment.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Divine protection, security, or salvation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Salvation as God's covenantal enclosure and protective presence.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus admits protection and security broadly as positive wall symbolism, whereas the book concentrates the positive symbol on covenant salvation and treats self-made security as counterfeit. It does not address many other recognized wall uses, such as social separation, self-control, or obstacles.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book preserves the consensus's central divine-protection/salvation identification but gives it a narrower covenantal formulation.\",\"rationale\":\"Isaiah 60:18 and Zechariah 2:5 are central consensus texts and directly support the book's positive identification. Proverbs 18:11 and Ezekiel 13:10–15 support its contrast with imagined wealth-based security and false peace. The difference is therefore one of extension and precision rather than replacement of the central referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts clearly provide examples of salvation, divine protection, and counterfeit security, but they do not by themselves prove that all positive wall imagery should be restricted to 'the covenant's enclosure.'\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
